        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"gissing-the-odd-women-7c5fd4b2db052397\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Rhoda directly presents as true that she has no loving feelings for Everard. The negative polarity does not alter its fundamentally truth-conditional mode. Although it pragmatically rejects his courtship, the highlighted utterance does not itself undertake a relationship or commitment. Her painful delivery supplies emotional context but no positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Rhoda’s strained manner and the surrounding indications of attraction leave the denial’s sincerity somewhat uncertain, but possible deception or self-deception does not change its truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rhoda directly tells Everard, in response to his declaration and proposal, “I don’t love you in the least.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The denial answers Everard’s demand for plain, honest words after he asks whether she can love him in return. It functions contextually as a rejection of his courtship and is reinforced by her statement that even loving him would not induce her to share his life. Her unfamiliar, apparently pained voice and the narration’s depiction of bodily agitation complicate the denial’s sincerity, while her commitment to her work helps explain the force of her resistance. None of this converts the core denial into a performative undertaking or expressive reflex.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I don’t love you in the least.”\",\"supports\":\"A maximally explicit present-tense denial of loving feeling, strongly supporting truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Everard asks, “Can you love me in return? ... Speak to me as one human being to another, plain, honest words.”\",\"supports\":\"Frames Rhoda’s utterance as a direct answer and avowal concerning her emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“And if I did I would never share your life.”\",\"supports\":\"Separates the denial of love from her refusal of a shared life, showing that the highlighted love clause itself is not chiefly a commitment-making act.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“The voice was very unlike her familiar tones. It seemed to hurt her to speak.”\",\"supports\":\"Shows emotional strain but does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rhoda says, “I have my work, and no one shall ever persuade me to abandon it.”\",\"supports\":\"Explains the contextual firmness of her rejection and the possible tension between feeling and chosen life course.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a negative-present form in Chapter XVII of George Gissing’s The Odd Women.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the classified expression is an explicit, present-tense negative equivalent of “I love you.”\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core force as a direct truth-conditional denial. Emotional strain, possible self-deception, and its courtship-rejecting function are contextual complications rather than missing core-force dimensions.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"Negative polarity concerns the represented content—absence of love—not the utterance’s classification as truth-conditional.\"}"
        }
      ],
